{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2333 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "96fd7d58909f7b1e241b5fe81de493f8e1f8cd86d3bf61b9947555a0b7514f9a" :edb/db-before-t 2332 :edb/db-after-t 2333 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535645 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:22:22.748Z" 13194139535645 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359076 13194139535645 true] [17592186229102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359087 13194139535645 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359062 13194139535645 true] [17592186229103 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359069 13194139535645 true] [17592186229656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535645 true] [17592186229656 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(1-t)^{2}})f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186229656 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$y = x(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dx})$.\n\nIf $y^{′}(x) = t$, then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t))$, where\n$y(t) = ((\\ln \\mid t \\mid - t + C)/((1 - t)^{2}))f(t) + g(t)$\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t) + g(t)$.\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n$\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{2x}{t - 1} = (1)/(t(1 - t))$" 13194139535645 false] [17592186229656 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1}=\\frac{1}{t(1-t)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2/(t-1)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t-1|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(t-1)^{2}\\frac{2x}{t-1}=(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(1-t)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-(t-1)^{2}\\frac{1}{t(t-1)} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=-\\frac{t-1}{t} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(t-1)x=\\frac{1-t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2}) & =\\frac{1-t}{t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x(t-1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\frac{1-t}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}-1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x(t-1)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+t,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-t+C}{(t-1)^{2}}){t}^{2}+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)={t}^{2},g(t)=t,$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)={t}^{2}+t.\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359063 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1089 "q-337654" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+f(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find the function $f(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1113 #uuid "3f42ef78-77b3-5413-802e-b393932da492" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359062 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x=-\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t),\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}t}={e}^{t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3}) & =-\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, let $u=\\frac{t}{3}.$ Then $t=3u$ and $\\text{d}t=3\\,\\text{d}u,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (3u){e}^{u}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9\\int u{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =9(u-1){e}^{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back $u=\\frac{t}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t/3}\\,\\text{d}t & =9(\\frac{t}{3}-1){e}^{t/3} \\\\  & =(3t-9){e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{t/3} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(3t-9){e}^{t/3}+C \\\\  & =(-4t+12){e}^{t/3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx=-4t+12+C{e}^{-t/3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{-t/3}+12-4t\n$$\n\nand finally, $f(t)=12-4t.$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359064 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359065 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359066 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359067 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359068 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1041 "selection" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359066 13194139535645 true] [17592186359063 1110 #uuid "88b6c6e7-636e-5fa6-b403-c5a3a19a0818" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359064 1034 "f(t)=12+4t" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359064 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359064 1111 #uuid "a09f4695-87c0-5c59-99a3-e27d7bdf5ec9" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359065 1034 "f(t)=4-12t" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359065 1111 #uuid "387a3d7d-f0d8-5fdf-a188-04d35defaf8b" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359066 1034 "f(t)=12-4t" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359066 1111 #uuid "5fd596b3-3a69-5f6f-bc6c-b6921485bd2f" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359067 1034 "f(t)=9-4t" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359067 1111 #uuid "09329e11-f928-5aaa-a6a0-bb64530cfe3d" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359068 1034 "f(t)=12-2t" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359068 1111 #uuid "05db37f1-42d3-5c07-bb56-b7d101ef6064" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359070 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1089 "q-337850" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3)+2{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t),y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t)=(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)f(t)+g(t)\n$$\n\nand $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Find $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\n*You may also wish to make use of the following result (constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t=(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1113 #uuid "84fc93c7-80a7-5e68-b1f8-c5227f556ef8" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359069 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nWe're told that, under the substitution ${y}^{′}(x)=t,$ our d’Alembert differential equation transforms to the first-order linear equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{4}{3}t.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this equation using the method of integrating factors. Since this equation is already of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nwe compute our integrating factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int P(t)\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our differential equation by $I(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t/3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting the left-hand side as an exact derivative, and integrating both sides of the equation with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3}) & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{-t/3} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x{e}^{-t/3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int t{e}^{-t/3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ x{e}^{-t/3} & =\\frac{4}{3}(-3t-9){e}^{-t/3}+C \\\\ x & =-4t-12+C{e}^{t/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\nx(t)=C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12.\n$$\n\nTo recover $y$, we substitute $x$ into\n\n$$\ny=x(t-3)+2{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =(C{e}^{t/3}-4t-12)(t-3)+2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $f(t)=t-3,\\text{ }g(t)=2{t}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-3.\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359071 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359072 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359073 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359074 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359075 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1041 "selection" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359074 13194139535645 true] [17592186359070 1110 #uuid "98880f9b-7288-54cb-b393-8a8764e9b39f" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359071 1034 "2{t}^{2}+t+3" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359071 1111 #uuid "bd5349ef-ab8b-5b64-b1c5-a734c15eac7b" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359072 1034 "2{t}^{2}+t-6" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359072 1111 #uuid "6872f006-57d3-5b87-ac29-7b4e2e40b21a" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359073 1034 "-2{t}^{2}+t-3" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359073 1111 #uuid "ebcda567-04a9-5e13-b5b3-4550b06adfc4" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359074 1034 "2{t}^{2}+t-3" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359074 1111 #uuid "45b6ea67-9d1d-5db0-a578-d047204f9fd1" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359075 1034 "2{t}^{2}-t-3" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359075 1111 #uuid "9bf805d4-81bd-5a66-8284-b6298da82573" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359077 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359082 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1089 "q-365372" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1100 "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation, defined for $t>0:$\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $t=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ the equation can be transformed to a first-order linear equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the functions $P$ and $Q.$\n\n$\\,\\,P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,Q(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1113 #uuid "f1849fca-686f-575a-b1ad-f816fd7436dc" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359076 1145 "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nIn our case, we wish to transform the equation\n\n$$\ny=x{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\ninto a first-order linear equation for $x=x(t).$ We proceed in three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Substitute, and differentiate with respect to $x.$\n\nWe have the substitution\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=x{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2}+3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum and product rules and using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({t}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3t) \\\\ t & ={t}^{2}+2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Isolate the derivative and take its reciprocal.\n\nWe isolate $\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-{t}^{2} & =2xt\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}+3\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ t(1-t) & =(2xt+3)\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ \\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{t(1-t)}{2xt+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by taking the reciprocal of each side, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Express the derivative as a first-order linear equation.\n\nFinally, we write this in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+P(t)x=Q(t)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2xt+3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2xt+3 \\\\ t(1-t)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-2xt & =3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2xt}{t(1-t)} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{2x}{1-t} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2x}{t-1} & =\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{2}{t-1},\\,Q(t)=\\frac{3}{t(1-t)}.\n$$" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359078 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359079 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359080 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359081 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1041 "field-1" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359079 13194139535645 true] [17592186359077 1110 #uuid "b3a7fbc8-7b63-5b5f-a8c7-291512e0531d" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359078 1034 "\\frac{1}{t-3}" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359078 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359078 1111 #uuid "f3a3fab5-e427-5224-8ae4-cf9255d02671" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359079 1034 "\\frac{2}{t-1}" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359079 1111 #uuid "4276ec8e-9b01-5466-9efe-dc1e655463f2" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359080 1034 "\\frac{2}{t(t-1)}" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359080 1111 #uuid "94fd8747-fd34-5774-902b-b69b91156e3d" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359081 1034 "\\frac{3}{t-1}" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535645 true] [17592186359081 1111 #uuid "e3c1b8d5-a84c-515a-a9ed-6a8abf2aec2f" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359083 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359084 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359085 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359086 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1041 "field-2" 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359084 13194139535645 true] [17592186359082 1110 #uuid "00c1a548-3cbc-547e-abc4-ddce42e5615a" 13194139535645 true] [175921863590